PUBLIC RESPONSE: Q – IV

IV is the cornerstone of safe care in this setting. A patient who has been on high-dose opioids for cancer pain and who is now in remission will have opioid-induced physical dependence and often significant psychological dependence, conditioned responses and residual pain concerns. A safe taper after remission requires a formal assessment of both the physical and the psychological contributors to ongoing opioid requirement and to the difficulty weaning, and an individualized, integrated taper plan.

A multidisciplinary pain management + psychiatry/ addiction medicine consultation allows:
* a careful review of the current opioid dose, duration of exposure and prior taper attempts;
* screening for opioid use disorder, mood, anxiety, insomnia and trauma related to cancer illness;
* an agreed, slow and flexible opioid dose reduction schedule with predefined hold criteria;
* the addition of non-opioid, multimodal pain and symptom management and psychosocial support rather than abrupt cessation or a medication switch.

That is why rapid opioid cessation in statement III is inappropriate, and a transition to methadone II is not indicated for an opioid-taper after remission. Buprenorphine-naloxone V is FDA approved for opioid use disorder and is not indicated for iatrogenic opioid dependence / cancer remission tapering and would introduce an unnecessary opioid agonist with risk of precipitated withdrawal and confusion.

Gradual dose reduction is a component of the integrated plan that will be produced by the multidisciplinary team. The stand-alone statement I “maintain on the current opioid regimen ... without introducing new medications to avoid potential side effects” is too restrictive, because an integrated taper normally includes non-opioid analgesic and psychosocial interventions. The essential, non-negotiable first step is IV, and in practice that plan will incorporate a slow taper as in I.

